\subsection{Definitions and mathematical statement}
\paragraph{Shared notation.}Standard number systems are $\mathbb N,\mathbb Z,\mathbb Q,\mathbb R,\mathbb C$. Specialized notation and index ranges follow the cited source and the definitions below.


Computability of a real-valued map depends on effective encodings of its inputs and outputs. A noisy-sample algorithm must specify its data representation, noise bounds, and the norm and tolerance of the approximation it returns.
\paragraph{Record scope and qualifications.}
The catalogue title is retained as metadata. Named certificates, contracts, bounds or frameworks in that title are not asserted as results of the original source extract.
No ground-truth map, approximation class, noise model, or specific approximation theorem is identified in the supplied record.

Source-record limitation: The source raises computability questions but does not identify a ground-truth map, function class, sampling representation, noise model, or approximation theorem. Even with its stated Turing and worst-case uniform-norm context, the supplied record has no specified computational problem whose decidability can be assessed faithfully.
\paragraph{Statement recorded in the supplied source.}
For a learning problem specified from point samples, is its ground-truth map computable; are the neural networks supplied by approximation theorems computable; and is there an algorithm that maps noisy samples to such a neural-network approximation? \href{https://doi.org/10.4171/owr/2021/55}{[S2]}
\paragraph{Source status.}
The supplied catalogue labels this source record open; that metadata was checked against the supplied snapshot on 2026-10-11. The label is preserved for provenance and is not an independent verification of present open status or mathematical correctness. The catalogue literature assessment is dated 2026-08-22.

